{"question": "What are the primary drawbacks associated with MRI scans?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The drawbacks of MRI scans include their much higher cost, patient discomfort due to being enclosed in a metal tube-like device for up to thirty minutes, significant noise levels that can cause anxiety, and the inability of patients with iron-containing metallic implants to undergo the scan.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0041-03"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 1.7. Medical Imaging*, p.31"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-008"}
{"question": "What component acts as a stable linker between the DNA template and the nascent RNA strands during prokaryotic transcription?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The RNA polymerase acts as a stable linker between the DNA template and the nascent RNA strands to ensure that elongation is not interrupted prematurely.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0407-03"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 15.2. Prokaryotic Transcription*, p.395"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-009"}
{"question": "What structural characteristic of white matter allows it to be easily distinguished from gray matter in magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scans?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "White matter is fatty due to the presence of myelin, whereas gray matter is not. This difference causes fat tissue and water to appear as different shades between black and white in MRI images, allowing the two types of matter to be easily distinguished.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0517-05"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 12.1. Basic Structure and Function of the Nervous System*, p.507"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-010"}
{"question": "How does the location of the cytoplasm and nucleus differ in a Schwann cell compared to an oligodendrocyte in relation to the myelin sheath?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "For Schwann cells, the outermost layer of the cell membrane contains the cytoplasm and the nucleus as a bulge on one side of the myelin sheath. In contrast, for oligodendrocytes, the rest of the cell is separate from the myelin sheath as a cell process extends back toward the cell body.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0527-09"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 12.2. Nervous Tissue*, p.517"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-011"}
{"question": "What is the function of the protein complex FACT during eukaryotic transcription?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The FACT complex pulls histones away from the DNA template as the RNA polymerase moves along it to allow polynucleotide synthesis. Once the pre-mRNA is synthesized, the FACT complex replaces the histones to recreate the nucleosomes.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0413-00"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 15.4. RNA Processing in Eukaryotes*, p.401"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-012"}
{"question": "What are the three factors that tRNA molecules must interact with?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "tRNA molecules must interact with three factors: 1) they must be recognized by the correct aminoacyl synthetase, 2) they must be recognized by ribosomes, and 3) they must bind to the correct sequence in mRNA.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0418-02"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 15.5. Ribosomes and Protein Synthesis*, p.406"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-013"}
{"question": "How do chemical modifications such as the addition of acetyl groups affect the charge of histone proteins and the tightness of DNA winding?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "When unmodified, histone proteins have a large positive charge, but adding chemical modifications like acetyl groups makes the charge less positive. This change in charge alters how tightly wound the DNA molecule is around the histone proteins.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0435-01"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 16.3. Eukaryotic Epigenetic Gene Regulation*, p.423"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-014"}
{"question": "What is the primary structural connection between the cerebellum and the brain stem?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The pons serves as the main connection between the cerebellum and the brain stem, visible on the anterior surface of the brain stem as a thick bundle of white matter.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0574-12"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 13.2. The Central Nervous System*, p.564"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-015"}
