{"question": "What happens to the plasma that does not diffuse back into the capillaries near the venules?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The remaining 15% of blood plasma drains out from the interstitial fluid into nearby lymphatic vessels. This lymph fluid passes through lymph nodes to be filtered by white blood cells before returning to the heart.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p1192-03"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 40.4. Blood Flow and Blood Pressure Regulation*, p.1180"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-018"}
{"question": "What specific cells from the endosteum create the internal callus after a bone fracture?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Chondrocytes from the endosteum create the internal callus by secreting a fibrocartilaginous matrix between the two ends of the broken bone.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0250-02"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 6.5. Fractures: Bone Repair*, p.240"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-019"}
{"question": "How do loop diuretics affect the reabsorption of ions in the loop of Henle and what is the resulting side effect?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Loop diuretics inhibit the reabsorption of Na+ and Cl- ions by the ascending limb of the loop of Henle. A side effect of this inhibition is an increase in urination.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p1210-04"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 41.2. The Kidneys and Osmoregulatory Organs*, p.1198"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-020"}
