{"question": "How do natural killer cells identify intracellular infections, such as those caused by viruses?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Natural killer cells identify intracellular infections by detecting the altered expression or reduced levels of major histocompatibility class (MHC) I molecules on the surface of infected cells.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p1230-05"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 42.1. Innate Immune Response*, p.1218"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-026"}
{"question": "What are the two main structural parts of an intervertebral disc, and what are their respective compositions?", "type": "factual", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Each intervertebral disc consists of an outer layer called the anulus fibrosus, which is a tough, fibrous layer firmly anchored to adjacent vertebral bodies, and an inner part called the nucleus pulposus, which consists of a softer, more gel-like material with a high water content.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0296-10"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 7.3. The Vertebral Column*, p.286"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify the passage answers this and that retrieval is required.", "id": "fact-027"}
