{"question": "What process occurs when homologous chromosomes align during meiosis to exchange segments of genetic material, and how do nonparental genotypes like Ab and aB arise from this mechanism?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "During meiosis, homologous chromosomes undergo synapsis and reciprocal physical exchanges of genetic material in a process called homologous recombination or crossing over. Because maternal and paternal alleles are combined onto the same chromosome during this crossover event, new allele combinations are created. Consequently, genotypes such as Ab and aB are nonparental types that result from this recombination.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0359-02", "biology-p0346-08"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 13.1. Chromosomal Theory and Genetic Linkage*, p.347", "Biology, 12.3. Laws of Inheritance*, p.334"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-001"}
{"question": "What specialized cellular structures connect adjacent cardiac muscle cells and support their synchronized contraction to help pump blood?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Cardiac muscle cells are connected to one another at their ends by specialized junctions called intercalated discs. These critical structures, formed where the sarcolemmas of adjacent cells bind together, support the synchronized contraction of the muscle, allowing the heart to work as a pump.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0441-00", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0856-01"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 10.7. Cardiac Muscle Tissue*, p.431", "Anatomy and Physiology, 19.2. Cardiac Muscle and Electrical Activity*, p.846"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-002"}
{"question": "What specific developmental and structural condition of the ovaries and reproductive anatomy would you expect in an individual with triplo-X syndrome based on the genetic requirement for ovary maturation and typical female developmental pathways?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "An individual with triplo-X syndrome has three X chromosomes and no Y chromosome, meaning they lack the SRY gene required to develop testes and will therefore develop into a phenotypically female individual. However, the proper maturation of female ovaries requires X chromosomes to reactivate, and while excess X chromosomes undergo inactivation to form Barr bodies, triplo-X is associated with reduced fertility and developmental delays.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0367-06", "anatomy-and-physiology-p1319-00"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 13.2. Chromosomal Basis of Inherited Disorders*, p.355", "Anatomy and Physiology, 27.3. Development of the Male and Female Reproductive Systems*, p.1309"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology", "biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-003"}
{"question": "What structural feature present in the walls of visceral organs allows cells to be electrically coupled and function together during the stress-relaxation response triggered by stretching?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Visceral organs contain single-unit smooth muscle, which has its muscle fibers joined by gap junctions so that the muscle contracts as a single unit. This organization allows them to exhibit the stress-relaxation response, where stretching the muscle triggers a contraction that is immediately followed by relaxation.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0444-04", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0445-00"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 10.8. Smooth Muscle*, p.434", "Anatomy and Physiology, 10.9. Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue*, p.435"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-004"}
{"question": "Which nitrogenous base found in the genetic material discovered to be transferred by Frederick Griffith pairs with adenine according to Erwin Chargaff's rules?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Frederick Griffith demonstrated the transfer of hereditary information, which is known to be DNA. According to Erwin Chargaff's rules, the amount of adenine in DNA equals the amount of thymine. Therefore, the nitrogenous base that pairs with adenine is thymine.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0376-00", "biology-p0378-01"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 14.1. Historical Basis of Modern Understanding*, p.364", "Biology, 14.2. DNA Structure and Sequencing*, p.366"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-005"}
{"question": "When skeletal muscle cells undergo extensive damage that exceeds the repair capacity of their local stem cells, what specific type of tissue replaces them, and how does this replacement tissue fundamentally differ in its cellular structure regarding myoblast fusion origins?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "When damage exceeds satellite cell repair, skeletal muscle fibers are replaced by scar tissue through fibrosis, as described in Passage A. Unlike the original functional skeletal muscle fibers described in Passage B, which are formed during early development by the fusion of multiple embryonic myoblasts into multinucleated cells with multiple copies of genes, the replacement scar tissue lacks this specialized contractile architecture and cannot contract.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0446-02", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0418-03"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 10.9. Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue*, p.436", "Anatomy and Physiology, 10.2. Skeletal Muscle*, p.408"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-006"}
{"question": "Which specific type of prokaryotic cell is infected by the lytic bacteriophage T4, and what radioactive isotope would tag this virus's genetic material?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Passage B states that the lytic bacteriophage T4 infects Escherichia coli, which are prokaryotic bacteria found in the human intestinal tract. Passage A explains that bacteriophage DNA is tagged with radioactive phosphorus (32P) because phosphorus is found in DNA but not in protein.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0378-00", "biology-p0556-04"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 14.2. DNA Structure and Sequencing*, p.366", "Biology, 21.2. Virus Infections and Hosts*, p.544"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-007"}
{"question": "What structural classification of nitrogenous base, defined by its single-ring structure as described in Passage B, is found in the template strands that form complementary base pairs according to Chargaff's rules?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Passage B states that pyrimidines are smaller nitrogenous bases with a single six-membered ring structure, including cytosine and thymine, which form complementary base pairs with purines such as adenine and guanine. Passage A explains that during DNA replication, each of the two strands of the double helix serves as a template from which new strands are copied to be complementary to the parental strand.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0387-01", "biology-p0378-01"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 14.3. Basics of DNA Replication*, p.375", "Biology, 14.2. DNA Structure and Sequencing*, p.366"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-008"}
{"question": "What is the Latin or Greek translation for the prefix found in the anatomical term for a body position characterized by a face-up orientation?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "A face-up body orientation is described as supine. The prefix of supine is 'sub', which is derived from Latin and means 'under' as outlined in the muscle root mnemonic table.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0460-01", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0033-01"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 11.2. Naming Skeletal Muscles*, p.450", "Anatomy and Physiology, 1.6. Anatomical Terminology*, p.23"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-009"}
{"question": "Which specific enzyme is responsible for removing the RNA primer during prokaryotic DNA replication, and what structural feature does this primer originally provide to allow DNA polymerase to begin adding nucleotides?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "RNA primase synthesizes an RNA primer that provides a free 3'-OH end, which is required because DNA polymerase cannot add nucleotides without it. Subsequently, DNA pol I utilizes its exonuclease activity to remove this RNA primer during replication.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0388-01", "biology-p0390-00"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 14.4. DNA Replication in Prokaryotes*, p.376", "Biology, 14.5. DNA Replication in Eukaryotes*, p.378"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-010"}
{"question": "Which specific skull bones that form the rigid anterior wall of the palate also provide an origin point for the primary muscle responsible for closing the mouth during mastication?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The anterior region of the palate (the hard palate) is created by the maxillary and palatine bones of the skull. The masseter muscle serves as the prime mover to close the mouth and elevate the mandible. The origin point for this masseter muscle includes the maxillary arch and zygomatic arch, meaning the maxilla is one of the key bones connecting these two regions.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0467-04", "anatomy-and-physiology-p1107-02"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 11.3. Axial Muscles of the Head, Neck, and Back*, p.457", "Anatomy and Physiology, 23.3. The Mouth, Pharynx, and Esophagus*, p.1097"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-011"}
{"question": "Which specific enzyme is responsible for removing RNA primers during eukaryotic DNA replication, given that eukaryotes use specialized polymerases for their strands and share a sliding clamp mechanism with prokaryotes?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Based on the provided passages, the text details the enzymes involved in eukaryotic replication, noting that pol delta and pol epsilon synthesize the strands and PCNA acts as the sliding clamp. Although passage B mentions that pol I removes primers in prokaryotes, the eukaryotic passage states that RNase H removes the RNA primer in eukaryotes, which is then replaced with DNA nucleotides.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0391-02", "biology-p0389-03"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 14.5. DNA Replication in Eukaryotes*, p.379", "Biology, 14.4. DNA Replication in Prokaryotes*, p.377"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-012"}
{"question": "What is the anatomical term for the large central body of the rectus abdominis muscles, and what connective tissue covering organizes the muscle fibers that form this structure?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The rectus abdominis muscles, commonly called the sit-up muscles, consist of a large central mass or belly that hypertrophies into 'six-pack abs' when exercised. The muscle fibers within this belly are bundled into units called fascicles, which are organized and covered by an additional layer of connective tissue called the perimysium.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0476-02", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0457-03"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 11.4. Axial Muscles of the Abdominal Wall and Thorax*, p.466", "Anatomy and Physiology, 11.1. Interactions of Skeletal Muscles, Their Fascicle Arrangement, and Their Lever Systems*, p.447"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-013"}
{"question": "If a pregnant woman over 36 is exposed to an environmental agent that causes a mutation where a pyrimidine is replaced by a purine in a gene, what specific type of substitution mutation is this, and what broader category of mutation does it belong to based on its origin?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Based on Passage B, a pregnant woman over 36 may experience environmental insults or mutations. Passage A explains that when a purine is replaced by a pyrimidine or vice versa, such as cytosine being replaced by adenine, it is known as a transversion substitution. Furthermore, Passage A states that mutations resulting from exposure to an environmental agent are classified as induced mutations.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0395-03", "anatomy-and-physiology-p1372-12"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 14.6. DNA Repair*, p.383", "Anatomy and Physiology, 28.7. Patterns of Inheritance*, p.1362"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology", "biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-014"}
{"question": "Which specific bone of the pectoral girdle that attaches the upper limb to the sternum of the axial skeleton contains an inferior surface where the subclavius muscle inserts to stabilize it during movement?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "According to the passages, the pectoral girdle consists of the scapula and the clavicle, with the clavicle attaching the upper limb to the sternum of the axial skeleton. The subclavius muscle is an anterior thorax muscle that stabilizes the clavicle during movement by depressing it, and its insertion is located on the inferior surface of the clavicle.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0481-00", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0318-00"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 11.5. Muscles of the Pectoral Girdle and Upper Limbs*, p.471", "Anatomy and Physiology, 8.1. The Pectoral Girdle*, p.308"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-015"}
{"question": "If a specific DNA gene contains the triplet CAC, what is the exact term for the corresponding complementary three-nucleotide sequence on the single-stranded messenger RNA that leaves the nucleus to specify the amino acid during protein synthesis?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "According to the passages, DNA uses a three-letter base code called a triplet, such as CAC, to specify a particular amino acid. During transcription, the genetic instructions on DNA are transcribed into a single-stranded messenger RNA molecule where thymine is replaced by uracil and adenine pairs with it. The resulting three-nucleotide sequences on this mRNA that specify each amino acid are called codons.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0403-02", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0120-01"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 15.1. The Genetic Code*, p.391", "Anatomy and Physiology, 3.4. Protein Synthesis*, p.110"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology", "biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-016"}
{"question": "Which specific intrinsic muscle group located on the dorsal side of the foot is responsible for extending the big toe, which contains only proximal and distal phalanx bones?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "The dorsal group of the intrinsic muscles of the foot includes the extensor digitorum brevis, which generally extends the toes. According to the bone structure of the lower limb, the big toe is toe number 1 and consists specifically of only two phalanx bones, the proximal and distal phalanges.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0499-05", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0345-09"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 11.6. Appendicular Muscles of the Pelvic Girdle and Lower Limbs*, p.489", "Anatomy and Physiology, 8.4. Bones of the Lower Limb*, p.335"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-017"}
{"question": "In eukaryotic transcription, which specific protein first binds to the TATA box, and what is the exact base sequence of the corresponding consensus sequence region found at the -10 position in prokaryotes?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "In eukaryotic transcription, the transcription factor TFIID is the first to bind to the TATA box. In prokaryotes, the consensus sequence at the -10 position upstream of the initiation site is TATAAT.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0406-02", "biology-p0437-01"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 15.2. Prokaryotic Transcription*, p.394", "Biology, 16.4. Eukaryotic Transcription Gene Regulation*, p.425"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-018"}
{"question": "What is the name of the nerve fiber bundle that extends from the optic chiasm to the brain, and which visual field information does it primarily transmit to the thalamus?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Based on Passage A, the optic nerve extends from the eye to the optic chiasm, where the structure continues as the optic tract. Passage B states that the optic tract extends from the optic chiasm and that the majority of its connections are to the thalamus—specifically, the lateral geniculate nucleus. Therefore, the bundle continuing past the chiasm is the optic tract, which transmits visual field information from both the right and left sides of the brain depending on whether the axons decussated or remained ipsilateral.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["anatomy-and-physiology-p0517-05", "anatomy-and-physiology-p0637-07"], "gold_pages": ["Anatomy and Physiology, 12.1. Basic Structure and Function of the Nervous System*, p.507", "Anatomy and Physiology, 14.2. Central Processing*, p.627"], "books": ["anatomy-and-physiology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-019"}
{"question": "How does the cellular structure of prokaryotes facilitate the concurrent synthesis of proteins from an mRNA transcript before termination is even complete?", "type": "multi_hop", "provenance": "llm_drafted_human_verified", "reference": "Prokaryotic cells lack membranous compartmentalization such as a nucleus, which allows transcription, translation, and mRNA degradation to occur in the same cytoplasm and in the same 5' to 3' direction. Because of this lack of a barrier, multiple ribosomes can concurrently translate the mRNA transcripts into polypeptides while the RNA polymerase is still elongating and before termination processes (such as rho-dependent or rho-independent termination) liberate the final mRNA transcript.", "gold_chunk_ids": ["biology-p0409-00", "biology-p0407-03"], "gold_pages": ["Biology, 15.3. Eukaryotic Transcription*, p.397", "Biology, 15.2. Prokaryotic Transcription*, p.395"], "books": ["biology"], "notes": "NEEDS REVIEW: verify BOTH passages are genuinely required.", "id": "multi-020"}
